{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788216331,"nonce":"AkGIrTngRpkZm2eQCUNZk8a8Kfc=","message":"WORKER REPORT (opus, own worktree, BORGES) — built to the ruling, unexecuted.\n\nBranch `issue/70ac258-things-stable-order`, two commits, `Source/AutoRimmer/ThingVerbs.cs`\nplus `accept/70ac258-things-stable-order.md`. `dotnet build -c Release`: succeeded,\n0 warnings, 0 errors; `Assemblies/` restored before committing, so the Build commit\nis still the orchestrator's to ship.\n\nWHAT SHIPPED\n\nThe `things` detail list and `FireScan` both got the pawns pattern, selection\nuntouched:\n\n  * `things {order:\"id\"|\"attention\"}` and `fires {order:…}` — the same argument\n    words `pawns` takes. Unknown order is a VerbArgsException naming `id|attention`,\n    never a silent fallback.\n  * detail list: `things_order` (`id-asc` default / `attention-desc`),\n    `things_selected_by` (always `attention-desc`), `attention_rank` per line.\n    Prefixed because the bare `order` in that same dictionary belongs to `rollups`.\n  * fire block: `order` (`id-asc` default / `outside-home-then-size-desc`),\n    `selected_by` (always `outside-home-then-size-desc`), `attention_rank` per line.\n  * the re-sort is survivors-only in both, after the cap; `attention_rank` is the\n    0-based position under the ranking computed BEFORE the cut.\n  * `things`' `order` argument reaches its own `data[\"fire\"]`, so both routes into\n    FireScan answer to one contract instead of two.\n  * `rollups` untouched. DigestVerb untouched.\n  * the class comment carries the honesty note: `order:\"id\"` stabilises the\n    SEQUENCE, not the MEMBERSHIP; above the cap the surviving set still moves,\n    `more` is the flag, a durable handle means raising the cap past the total or\n    holding the id.\n\nTHREE DIVERGENCES FROM THE PAWNS PATTERN, each with its reason\n\n1. **The fires' ranked view publishes `outside-home-then-size-desc` as `order`,\n   not `attention-desc`.** The ruling says `selected_by` there states its real\n   rule; when the caller asks for the ranked view, `order` describes that same\n   sequence, and giving one sequence two names would be worse than either name\n   alone. No ThingAttention is computed in FireScan, so \"attention-desc\" would be\n   a lie that reads as data. The ARGUMENT word stays `attention` — a caller who\n   learned the vocabulary on `pawns` must not have to learn a second one.\n   `attention_rank` also keeps its name there, for the same reason; the field is\n   the shared vocabulary, `selected_by` says what the ranking actually was.\n\n2. **`things_order`/`things_selected_by` are prefixed** where pawns publishes bare\n   `order`/`selected_by`. Forced: `Rollups` returns ONE dictionary carrying two\n   lists, and the unprefixed `order` was already taken by `rollups`. The existing\n   `things_order` key kept its name rather than being renamed.\n\n3. **`Rollups`' new `order` parameter is optional (`= OrderById`)**, because\n   `PlaceVerbs`' room `contains` calls it with `detail:false` and my territory is\n   ThingVerbs.cs. Nothing changes for that caller — with no detail list there is\n   nothing for an order to apply to.\n\nTWO INCIDENTAL CORRECTIONS, both from restructuring the detail branch into the\npawns shape and both worth knowing about:\n\n  * `ThingAttention` is now scored ONCE per thing instead of inside the sort\n    comparator. It reaches `IsForbidden` and the hp memo, so it was being\n    recomputed O(n log n) times, and a comparator that recomputes a live key can\n    in principle disagree with itself (`List.Sort` throws on that). `pawns` scores\n    into a `List\u003cKeyValuePair\u003cint,T\u003e\u003e` for the same reason; this is the pattern,\n    not a departure from it.\n  * null-def entries are dropped BEFORE the cut rather than inside it, where they\n    used to consume a `detail_cap` slot and emit nothing. Consequence to note:\n    `things_total`/`things_more` now count the scored candidates rather than the\n    raw pool, so total, `more` and the ranks all count one population. No shipped\n    call site can produce a null-def entry, so no live result changes.\n\nONE THING CONFIRMED THAT COMMENT #1 LISTED AS INFERRED\n\n`Fire.fireSize` does grow monotonically. `RimWorld/Fire.cs` `DoComplexCalcs`:\n`fireSize += 0.00055f * flammabilityMax * 150f * (1f - effectiveVacuumForFire)`,\nclamped at 1.75, with `fireSize = 0.1f` at construction. So the fire list's score\nmoves faster than either mood or hit points, which is the argument for including\nit. Cited in the class comment.\n\nACCEPTANCE — `accept/70ac258-things-stable-order.md`\n\nPinned per ruling point 2: step A0b reads `things {def:\u003cDEF\u003e, detail:true,\ndetail_cap:300}` and refuses to continue unless `things_more == 0`, so the\nstability check never runs on a truncated list.\n\nThe score change between the two reads is a `forbid` on the highest-id stack\n(`+100000` to ThingAttention) rather than a hauling tick — a deterministic\n~100000-point move with nothing added to or removed from the map, which is what\nthe acceptance asks for. The environmental version (advance 2500 ticks, assert on\nthe intersection in relative order) is kept as C3 and is explicitly not a\npass/fail gate. `detail_cap:1` with that stack forbidden is the cap check.\n\nFLAGGED FOR THE ORCHESTRATOR\n\n  * **D3, staging fires, is the step most likely to fail and I could not verify\n    it.** `dev:spawn-thing {def:\"Fire\"}` has to pass\n    `Verse/DebugThingPlaceHelper.IsDebugSpawnable`, which keys on\n    `ThingDef.category`; the Core def XML is not in the decompiled reference on\n    BORGES, only the C#. `Ethereal` passes the guard and Fire is very likely\n    Ethereal, but that is a recollection, not a reading. The step carries a\n    `force:true` retry and, if that fails too, says to stop rather than improvise —\n    vanilla's own route is `FireUtility.TryStartFireIn